HOW_TO_READ = ('<b>How to read a box.</b> Top: the class name (dashed border = interface; &laquo;enum&raquo; = a fixed '
 'list of values). Middle: its fields, the state it holds. Bottom: its methods. <b>The arrows.</b> Hollow triangle = '
 'implements. Filled diamond = owns, and the four coming off the left of the service are the only ownerships in the '
 'design: the service owns every order and every restaurant, an order owns its lines and its bill, a restaurant owns '
 'its menu. Plain arrow = references: an order points at its rider <i>by id</i>, because the rider outlives the order '
 'and belongs to the fleet. Dashed green = handed in through <code>configure()</code> or a setter &mdash; the '
 'pricing chain, the dispatcher, the gateway, the clock and the charge log, with the cancellation policy and the '
 'demand signal arriving the same way. Dotted blue = notifies. <b>Where the state lives, and where the locks are:</b> '
 'the restaurant has the menu, the stock counts and one <code>ReentrantLock</code>; each order has its own lock for its '
 'own state, and its rider field is an <code>AtomicReference</code> because two dispatchers can reach for it at once; a '
 'rider has a single <code>AtomicReference</code> flag and no lock at all; the service itself holds no lock, only maps '
 'and the rules it was handed. Notice what is <i>not</i> here: no Payment class (the gateway is an interface, and an '
 'order keeps only the charge id), no Notification class, and no separate Menu class &mdash; a menu is two maps inside '
 'the restaurant that owns them. Two classes in Main.java are deliberately not drawn: <code>FakeGateway</code> and '
 '<code>CustomerApp</code> are the stand-ins the demo and the tests hand in, not part of the design.')

# ============================================================ page 04: the code
CODE_INTRO = ('Read it with page 03 open in a second tab if you want the diagram beside it. The green comment above each '
 'class and method says what it does; read only those first for the shape, then the bodies for the mechanics. Each copy '
 'button copies that whole file for your IDE. Below Main.java: Extensions.java (every follow-up\'s reference code, with '
 'an <code>ExtDemo</code> main that runs all of it) and FailureTests.java (thirty-eight checks proven; '
 '<code>javac Main.java Extensions.java FailureTests.java &amp;&amp; java FailureTests</code> prints ALL PASS).')

# ============================================================ page 05: follow-ups and practice
IMPLEMENT_CARD = ('<div class="card"><div class="ch"><h3>0 &middot; Implement the system</h3>'
 '<button class="timer" data-min="60">start 60:00</button></div><div class="cb"><div class="prompt">' + PROMPT + '</div>'
 'Before typing, write your six to eight clarifying questions (one restaurant per cart, and whether payment is in '
 'scope, first); then type in the order of Main.java: OrderState with its transition table, PartnerState, Money, the '
 'records (Geo, MenuItem, OrderLine, Bill, PriceCtx, Refund), the seven one-method interfaces, the pricing rules with '
 'the two decorators, NearestFree and StandardCancellation, Restaurant with its lock and <code>reserve()</code>, Cart, '
 'Order with its own lock and its compare-and-set for the rider, DeliveryPartner with its compare-and-set, then '
 'OrderService with placeOrder in its numbered steps, then a main with thirty threads racing for ten portions.</div></div>')

FU = [
("Marketing wants 40% off, capped at 120 rupees, on a bill that already has a distance fee and a surge multiplier on it.",
 "functional", 10,
 "The price is a list of one-method rules applied in order, so this is two new classes and two lines. "
 "<code>PercentCoupon</code> takes its percentage off the food only; <code>CappedDiscount</code> wraps it and trims "
 "whatever it took off down to the cap, so &ldquo;40% off, up to 120&rdquo; is a wrapper rather than a clause inside "
 "every coupon written from now on. The order of the list is the order of the maths: food, distance fee, surge on the "
 "fee only, then the coupons &mdash; 558.00 of food, 44.00 of fee (20 plus 8 a kilometre, rounded up to 3 km), 22.00 "
 "of surge, and 223.20 of coupon trimmed to 120.00, for 504.00. Every rule is wrapped in <code>CheckedRule</code> "
 "before it is used, so one that leaves the parts and the total out of step throws before the card is touched.",
 sect(src, "final class DistanceFee", "final class CheckedRule")),
("Two customers tap for the last two portions at the same instant. Prove one of them loses, with a test.",
 "non-functional", 10,
 "The race lives between reading a stock count and writing it back. <code>Restaurant.reserve</code> does the whole "
 "read-modify-write inside the restaurant's lock, and in two phases: check <i>every</i> line against the live menu and "
 "the live stock first, and only then decrement them all, so an order is all of its lines or none of them. The proof is "
 "a count, not a hope: thirty threads wait on one latch, each wants two of the ten portions, and afterwards exactly "
 "five orders exist, the stock is exactly zero and the card was touched exactly five times. The last number is the one "
 "worth pointing at &mdash; it proves the twenty-five losers never reached the gateway at all.",
 T("        // 1. thirty phones", "        // 2. one unavailable line")),
("One lock per restaurant. Does that scale, or have you serialised Swiggy?",
 "non-functional", 5,
 "It scales, and the answer is arithmetic rather than opinion: about ten hash operations under the lock, roughly two "
 "microseconds, against four orders a minute in a busy kitchen. Even a fictional fifty checkouts a second into one "
