("The card is declined at checkout. What is the state of the system?",
 "functional", 10,
 "Exactly what it was. The order is built <i>after</i> the charge returns, so a decline leaves no order in the map, "
 "nothing on the restaurant's screen and no refund to issue. What it does leave is a reservation, so the catch hands "
 "the stock straight back under the restaurant's lock and rethrows; the customer sees one clean error and can try "
 "another card. The same exit covers a sold-out line and a pricing rule that throws. One thing to defend out loud: "
 "the card is charged with no lock held, because four hundred milliseconds inside a lock would stall every other "
 "customer of that restaurant. And read step 3 carefully &mdash; a decline and a gateway that never answers are not "
 "the same event, and the next question is about the second one.",
 sect(src, "private Order doPlace", "void accept(String orderId)")),
("The gateway times out. You do not know whether the customer was charged. Now what?",
 "functional", 10,
 "This is the one case &ldquo;pay, then commit&rdquo; cannot rule out, and the wrong answer is to treat it as a "
 "decline: the money may have left, and nothing points at it. So the attempt is written down <i>before</i> the "
 "gateway is called and marked UNKNOWN when the call dies; that row is the only thing left pointing at the "
 "customer's money. No order is created and the food goes back, exactly as for a decline. A sweep then asks the "
 "gateway what really happened, and if the money moved it is an orphan &mdash; an UNKNOWN charge can never belong to "
 "an order, because an order is only built after a charge that answered &mdash; so it goes back, and the answer is "
 "written into the log, which is what makes running the sweep twice refund once.",
