 + "\n" + sect(src, "Order order(String id)", "Cart openCart(")),
("His network drops on the Pay screen and the app sends the checkout again. Do not charge him twice.",
 "non-functional", 5,
 "The app sends a key with the tap &mdash; the same key on every retry of the same tap &mdash; and the service claims "
 "it with one <code>putIfAbsent</code> before doing anything else. The second arrival finds an attempt already there "
 "and <i>waits on it</i> rather than starting a second checkout: it gets the first attempt's order back, or the first "
 "attempt's exception. That waiting is the part people get wrong; returning &ldquo;already in progress&rdquo; pushes "
 "the problem back to an app that will simply retry again. A failed attempt removes its key, so a genuine retry after "
 "a declined card is allowed to charge. The test is a count: same key twice, one order, and the gateway touched once.",
 sect(src, "private static final class InFlight", "private Order doPlace")
 + "\n" + T("        int before = g6.chargeCount();", "        // 9. the restaurant rejects")),
("The customer cancels. Sometimes it is free, sometimes there is a fee, and sometimes it is too late.",
 "twist", 10,
 "Two rules, in two places, each where it belongs. <i>Whether</i> a cancel is allowed is the transition table: "
 "CANCELLED follows PLACED, ACCEPTED, PREPARING and READY but not PICKED_UP, so once the rider has the food the "
 "attempt throws instead of quietly succeeding. <i>How much comes back</i> is an injected policy, asked inside the "
 "order's lock and given the state it is cancelling out of, so a state change racing the refund cannot fool it: free "
 "before the kitchen starts, the total minus a flat fee once it is cooking. The stock follows the same logic &mdash; "
 "cancelled before ACCEPTED puts the food back, cancelled while cooking does not, because that food exists now. Then "
 "the refund, the queue removal and the rider hand-back all happen outside the lock.",
