 + "\n" + sect(src, "final class StandardCancellation", "final class FakeGateway")),
("Two orders become ready in the same millisecond and one rider is nearest to both. And what if nobody is free at all?",
 "non-functional", 10,
 "Ranking and claiming are deliberately separate. The rule ranks a <i>snapshot</i> of the free riders, which may "
 "already be stale when it returns &mdash; that is fine, because the winner is decided by <code>claim()</code>, one "
 "compare-and-set on the rider's flag, and the loser walks to the next candidate. There is a second claim most people "
 "miss: two dispatchers can be looking at the same waiting order and win two <i>different</i> riders, so filling the "
 "order's rider field is itself a compare-and-set, and whoever loses hands his rider straight back. Nobody free is a "
 "normal outcome, not an exception: the order joins a waiting deque. Two lines then decide whether the fleet works at "
 "all &mdash; queue the order <i>before</i> looking again, so a rider freed in the gap is not missed, and hand the "
 "rider back on every terminal path, or the fleet does one delivery each and dies.",
 sect(src, "boolean claim(String forOrderId)", "void release()")
 + "\n" + sect(src, "boolean markReady(String orderId)", "private boolean tryAssign")
 + "\n" + sect(src, "private boolean tryAssign", "void deliver(String orderId)")
 + "\n" + sect(src, "private void handBack(Order o)", "private void publish(")),
("When will it arrive? And where does the time come from, and where does live GPS go?",
 "design", 8,
 "The ETA is a pure function of state: what the kitchen still owes, plus the ride at a speed handed in. What the "
 "kitchen owes is measured from the <code>PREPARING</code> stamp, not from when the customer paid &mdash; an order "
 "sitting unaccepted for ten minutes still owes the full eighteen &mdash; which is what the order's stamp map is for. "
 "Time comes from one injected interface, so a test picks the instant, starts the cooking, moves the clock ten "
 "minutes and asserts the exact number of minutes left; nothing else in the system reads the wall clock. Live GPS is "
 "deliberately <i>not</i> an observer: state changes happen six times an hour, positions arrive a few times a second, "
 "so the location stream gets its own topic per order and touches no lock.",
 X("eta", "multi-restaurant basket") + "\n"
 + "// where time comes from: one interface, handed in, defaulted, never read from the wall clock inside a method\n"
 + "interface Clock { long nowMs(); }\n"
 + "private Clock clock = System::currentTimeMillis;\n"
 + "void setClock(Clock c) { clock = c; }\n\n"
 + "// in a test: pick the instant, start the kitchen, then move it\n"
 + "long[] now = { 1_700_000_000_000L };\n"
 + "svc.setClock(() -> now[0]);\n"
 + "svc.accept(id); svc.startPreparing(id);                // stamps PREPARING at now[0]\n"
 + "now[0] += 10 * 60_000;                                 // ten minutes on the tawa: eight left of eighteen\n\n"
 + X("live tracking", "persistence")),
("One tap, two restaurants: biryani from one and dessert from another.",
 "twist", 5,
 "It fans out into one order per restaurant, because stock, prep time and the rider all belong to a restaurant; a "
 "single order spanning two kitchens would have two prep times and two pickups and no honest state machine. So the "
 "basket places each order in turn with a key derived from the one tap. The interesting part is the failure: there is "
 "no distributed transaction here, so if the second restaurant is out of stock the first order is cancelled, which "
 "refunds it through the ordinary path. Say that out loud rather than hiding it &mdash; compensation is the answer, and "
 "the alternative (reserve everywhere, then pay once) is a two-phase commit you do not want in an hour.",
 X("multi-restaurant basket", "scheduled orders")),
("A rider accepts and then does not show. And can one rider carry two drops on the same trip?",
 "twist", 10,
 "Push assignment lies a little, so an offer holds the rider, asks him with a deadline, and hands him straight back on "
 "a no or on silence &mdash; the release happens before the method returns, so the pool never leaks. The refusal is "
 "recorded, and the ranking rule pushes a flaky rider down the list by charging him half a kilometre per miss, which is "
 "a new <code>AssignmentRule</code> and nothing else. Batching is the smaller change of the two: the rider's state was "
 "a flag with two values, so a compare-and-set fitted; with capacity it becomes a counter, so the same single "
 "indivisible claim becomes <code>Semaphore.tryAcquire</code>. The queue, the ranking and the terminal release do not "
 "move at all.",
 X("the rider says no", "a new pricing rule") + "\n" + X("pooling", "the rider says no")),
("Scheduled orders: 'deliver at eight', booked at four in the afternoon.",
 "twist", 5,
 "Not a new state. A booking sits outside the order model until the kitchen has to start, which is the requested time "
 "minus that restaurant's prep time; at that point the scheduler places an ordinary order and everything downstream is "
 "unchanged. The part worth pointing at is the id: it is derived from the booking, so a tick that runs twice, or two "
 "servers running the same job, place it exactly once &mdash; the same idempotency that makes a retried tap safe. What "
 "you should <i>not</i> do is reserve the stock at booking time; the kitchen will restock before then, and holding a "
 "portion for four hours is how a restaurant goes sold out at six.",
 X("scheduled orders", "pooling")),
("Persist it. And now there are two servers.",
 "twist", 10,
 "The order's lock only guards one process, so it becomes a conditional UPDATE and the database performs the same "
 "atomic step: <code>UPDATE orders SET state = ? WHERE id = ? AND state = ?</code>, where zero rows updated means "
 "another server moved it first &mdash; exactly the error the in-memory guard throws. The insert carries the "
 "idempotency key with ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING, so a retried tap still creates one order. Stock moves the same way: "
 "<code>SET left = left - ? WHERE left &gt;= ?</code>. The one genuinely new piece is the outbox: the notification is "
 "written as a row in the <i>same transaction</i> as the state change and drained by a separate sender, because a "
 "process that commits a state and then dies before the push is the one failure the in-memory try/catch cannot cover.",
 X("persistence", "Runs every extension")),
("Which pattern is where, why is the state an enum and not eight classes, and where would a Factory or a Builder earn its place?",
 "design", 8,
 "Not one of them was chosen up front; each is what a move produced, and moves 10 and 11 have the full table with the "
 "line of code for each. What that table does not answer is the enum question, which interviewers ask because the "
 "textbook State pattern is a class per state. Eight classes would buy nothing here: a state in this system has no "
 "behaviour of its own &mdash; no <code>PreparingState.handle()</code> &mdash; only a list of legal successors, so it "
 "is data, and data belongs in an <code>EnumMap</code> you can read in one screen and print in an error message. Same "
 "test for the other two: Factory earns its place when coupons arrive as config rows and somebody must turn a row "
 "into a rule; Builder earns its place when an order gains optional fields, because today all seven are required and "
 "a builder would only let you forget one.",
 "// Strategy: a rule behind an interface, handed in, never built by the service\n"
 "interface PricingRule { Bill apply(Bill running, PriceCtx ctx); }\n\n"
 "// Chain: the bill grows one rule at a time, in an order you can read off the list\n"
 "for (PricingRule rule : pricing) bill = rule.apply(bill, ctx);\n\n"
 "// Decorator, twice: one caps any coupon, one polices every rule including the ones written next year\n"
 "new CappedDiscount(new PercentCoupon(\"TASTY40\", 4000), Money.rupees(\"120.00\"));\n"
 "for (PricingRule r : pricing) checked.add(new CheckedRule(r));\n\n"
 "// State as DATA, not eight classes: the legal moves are a table, so an illegal move cannot be written\n"
 "NEXT.put(PICKED_UP, EnumSet.of(DELIVERED));            // on the road: no going back\n"
 "if (!from.canGoTo(to)) throw new IllegalStateException(o.id() + \": \" + from + \" -> \" + to\n"
 "                                                     + \" (legal: \" + from.next() + \")\");\n\n"
 "// Factory: not yet. The list IS the registry; it earns the name when coupons come from config rows\n"
 "List<PricingRule> fromConfig = rows.stream().map(PricingRules::of).toList();\n\n"
 "// Builder: not yet. Seven required fields is a constructor; a tip, a note and packaging would change that\n"
 "new Order(id, customerId, restaurantId, chargeId, lines, bill, dropAt, placedAtMs);\n"),
]

